==English==
[[File:Bunks in a Seven-Cent Lodging-House, Pell Street.jpeg|thumb|upright|Bunks in a '''flophouse''', circa 1890.]]

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|flop-house}}

===Etymology===
From {{compound|en|flop|house}}, originally [[hobo]] slang, presumably from slang {{m|en|flop||lie down to sleep}}.<ref name="oetyld">{{R:Online Etymology Dictionary}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/ˈflɒphaʊs/}}
* {{audio|en|En-au-flophouse.ogg|Audio (AU)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|US|slang}} A [[cheap]] [[hotel]] or [[boarding house]] where many people sleep in large rooms. {{defdate|from 20th c.}}
#* '''1904''', ''[[w:McClure's|McClure’s Magazine]],'' November 1904:<ref name="oetyld" />
#*: In one of [Cincinnati’s] slum districts stands the Silver Moon, a “'''flop house'''” (i.e., a house where the occupants are “flopped” out of their hanging bunks by letting down the ropes).
#* {{RQ:Kerouac On the Road|passage=We even visited some drunken seamen in a '''flophouse''' on Mission Street that she knew; they offered us whisky.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1982|author={{w|TC Boyle}}|title=Water Music|page=34|publisher=Penguin|year_published=2006
|passage=He was born out back of a twopenny '''flophouse''' in what the wags called “The Holy Land” […].}}

